# Large binary artifacts.
# Safety net: the directory rules below are the primary defence, but these
# extensions guarantee that tensors/arrays can never be staged from a path
# nobody anticipated (linear-probes/features alone is ~105 GB).
*.pt
*.npy
*.npz
*.pkl
*.pickle
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.h5
*.hdf5
*.ckpt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.joblib

# Large tabular/columnar data and local databases. Text-based TSV/CSV/JSON are
# intentionally kept trackable because this repository versions small datasets
# and evaluation results in those formats.
*.parquet
*.feather
*.arrow
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db

# Downloaded archives should be stored externally or recreated from their
# documented source rather than committed to Git.
*.zip
*.7z
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.tar.bz2
*.tbz2
*.tar.xz
*.txz

# data
modernbert_word_features_dev
modernbert_word_features_train
# Anchored to their real locations. Previously bare `features` / `experiments`,
# which matched ANY directory of that name at any depth -- a future source
# package called features/ would have silently vanished from the repo.
secondary/linear-probes/features/
# experiments/ keeps its config/result JSONs; the probe weights are caught by *.pt.
runs/

# Raw per-approach model dumps (probabilities/logits), regenerable from the
# scripts + tracked datasets. `data.json` alone missed the real filenames.
data.json
data_*.json
*-data.json

# generated per-dataset artifacts (deterministically rebuilt from the tracked TSV/M2)
datasets/**/*.sentences.json
datasets/**/*.gold.json

# Fine-tuning training splits and checkpoints.
# The ft* slices are 200k sentences x 5 languages (~175 MB of TSV) and the
# decoder pairs are comparable -- far too large to track, and both rebuild
# exactly from the offsets in their .provenance.json, which stays tracked.
# The eval5k/val5k/train50k *.tsv splits are deliberately NOT covered by this:
# the TSV view is the small benchmark view. Their heavy sibling views ARE
# ignored, by the synthetic-data block below -- the earlier claim that these
# splits are "small" held only for .tsv (c4200_train50k.jsonl alone is 705 MB).
datasets/synthetic-data/*/*_ft*.tsv
finetuning/data/*.pairs.tsv
finetuning/models/**
!finetuning/models/*/
!finetuning/models/*/train_meta.json
finetuning/decoder_ged/prepared/
# Per-family checkpoint dirs. Globbed rather than named one by one: encoder_ged/
# was added after decoder_ged/models/ was listed here and silently escaped every
# rule, leaking 24 x 33 MB tokenizer.json (589 MB) that the *.safetensors/*.bin
# extension rules do not match. This covers future families too.
finetuning/*/models/**
!finetuning/*/models/*/
!finetuning/*/models/*/train_meta.json
!finetuning/*/models/*/train.log

# Heavy synthetic-data views.
# Each split is written as five synchronized views from one build (see
# datasets/synthetic-data/README.md). The .tsv (GED benchmark view) and the
# .manifest.json (source file, line range, mERRANT commit, spaCy version, GED
# policy, counts) stay tracked; the three views below rebuild exactly from that
# manifest and total ~6.1 GB. The older `*_ft*.tsv` line above caught only the
# ft slices, so every train10k/20k/50k/100k + eval5k/val5k .jsonl/.m2/.pairs.tsv
# was still stageable.
datasets/synthetic-data/*/*.jsonl
datasets/synthetic-data/*/*.m2
datasets/synthetic-data/*/*.pairs.tsv
# Exception: the eval5k/val5k M2s are ~1 MB each (24 MB in total, against 431 MB
# for the training tiers) and they carry the error-type annotation behind the
# synthetic error-composition analysis. Keeping them means that table can be
# recomputed from a clone; the training-split M2s still cannot, and do not need
# to be -- nothing scores against them.

# Training frameworks and experiment trackers.
checkpoints/
lightning_logs/
wandb/
mlruns/
events.out.tfevents.*

# approach run outputs (raw model probs + adapted predictions), regenerable
outputs/
# NOTE: secondary/linear-probes/output/ (singular) is deliberately NOT ignored -- it holds
# small per-layer metric CSVs that are results worth keeping. Beware the
# near-collision with the outputs/ rule above when adding new write paths.

# Final MultiGED TSV bundles are reproducible from raw outputs + thresholds.json.
test-predictions/submissions/

# Frozen FineWeb/FineWeb-2 document samples the frequency tables are counted
# from (~1 GB). Redrawn exactly from the pinned revision + shard/row-group
# stride recorded in each <language>.manifest.json, which stays tracked.
secondary/token-frequencies/samples/*.jsonl.gz

# Corpora are never committed. The learner corpora are licensed and fetched by
# hand (see datasets/README.md); the C4200 synthetic splits are downloaded from
# Hugging Face; MLCheck splits are rebuilt locally. Only manifests, READMEs,
# licences and build code are tracked.
datasets/multiged-2023/*/*.tsv
datasets/multiged-2023/*/*.m2
datasets/multiged-2023/*/*.orig
datasets/write-and-improve/english/
datasets/write-and-improve/source/
datasets/synthetic-data/*/*.tsv
datasets/synthetic-data/sources/
# The Python template's bare `build/` would otherwise hide the dataset builders.
!datasets/synthetic-data/build/
.venv-data/
